s as follows:\n\n\"This is an example sentence supported by multiple data references [Data: <dataset name> (record ids); <dataset name> (record ids)].\"\n\nDo not list more than 5 record ids in a single reference. Instead, list the top 5 most relevant record ids and add \"+more\" to indicate that there are more.\n\nFor example:\n\"Person X is the owner of Company Y and subject to many allegations of wrongdoing [Data: Reports (1), Entities (5, 7); Relationships (23); Claims (7, 2, 34, 64, 46, +more)].\"\n\nwhere 1, 5, 7, 23, 2, 34, 46, and 64 represent the id (not the index) of the relevant data record.\n\nDo not include information where the supporting evidence for it is not provided.\n\nLimit the total report length to 2500 words.\n\n# Example Input\n-----------\nText:\n\nEntities\n\nid,entity,description\n5,VERDANT OASIS PLAZA,Verdant Oasis Plaza is the location of the Unity March\n6,HARMONY ASSEMBLY,Harmony Assembly is an organization that is holding a march at Verdant Oasis Plaza\n\nRelationships\n\nid,source,target,description\n37,VERDANT OASIS PLAZA,UNITY MARCH,Verdant Oasis Plaza is the location of the Unity March\n38,VERDANT OASIS PLAZA,HARMONY ASSEMBLY,Harmony Assembly is holding a march at Verdant Oasis Plaza\n39,VERDANT OASIS PLAZA,UNITY MARCH,The Unity March is taking place at Verdant Oasis Plaza\n40,VERDANT OASIS PLAZA,TRIBUNE SPOTLIGHT,Tribune Spotlight is reporting on the Unity march taking place at Verdant Oasis Plaza\n41,VERDANT OASIS PLAZA,BAILEY ASADI,Bailey Asadi is speaking at Verdant Oasis Plaza about the march\n43,HARMONY ASSEMBLY,UNITY MARCH,Harmony Assembly is organizing the Unity March\n\nOutput:\n{\n    \"title\": \"Verdant Oasis Plaza and Unity March\",\n    \"summary\": \"The community revolves around the Verdant Oasis Plaza, which is the location of the Unity March. The plaza has relationships with the Harmony Assembly, Unity March, and Tribune Spotlight, all of which are associated with the march event.\",\n    \"rating\": 5.0,\n    \"rating_explanation\": \"The impact severity rating is moderate due to the potential for unrest or conflict during the Unity March.\",\n    \"findings\": [\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Verdant Oasis Plaza as the central location\",\n            \"explanation\": \"Verdant Oasis Plaza is the central entity in this community, serving as the location for the Unity March. This plaza is the common link between all other entities, suggesting its significance in the community. The plaza's association with the march could potentially lead to issues such as public disorder or conflict, depending on the nature of the march and the reactions it provokes. [Data: Entities (5), Relationships (37, 38, 39, 40, 41,+more)]\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Harmony Assembly's role in the community\",\n            \"explanation\": \"Harmony Assembly is another key entity in this community, being the organizer of the march at Verdant Oasis Plaza. The nature of Harmony Assembly and its march could be a potential source of threat, depending on their objectives and the reactions they provoke. The relationship between Harmony Assembly and the plaza is crucial in understanding the dynamics of this community. [Data: Entities(6), Relationships (38, 43)]\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Unity March as a significant event\",\n            \"explanation\": \"The Unity March is a significant event taking place at Verdant Oasis Plaza. This event is a key factor in the community's dynamics and could be a potential source of threat, depending on the nature of the march and the reactions it provokes. The relationship between the march and the plaza is crucial in understanding the dynamics of this community. [Data: Relationships (39)]\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Role of Tribune Spotlight\",\n            \"explanation\": \"Tribune Spotlight is reporting on the Unity March taking place in Verdant Oasis Plaza. This suggests that the event has attracted media attention, which could amplify its impact on the community. The role of Tribune Spotlight could be significant in shaping public perception of the event and the entities involved. [Data: Relationships (40)]\"\n        }\n    ]\n}\n\n\n# Real Data\n\nUse the following text for your answer. Do not make anything up in your answer.\n\nText:\n-----Entities-----\nhuman_readable_id,title,description,degree\r\n1318,ROOT MEAN SQUARE ERROR,\"**Root Mean Square Error (RMSE)**\n\n**Definition and Purpose**:  \nRoot Mean Square Error (RMSE) is a statistical measure that quantifies the differences between values predicted by a model and the values that are actually observed. It serves as a key indicator of the accuracy of a predictive model, providing insight into how well the model performs in terms of prediction error.\n\n**Mathematical Formulation**:  \nThe RMSE is calculated using the following formula:\n\n\\[\n\\text{RMSE} = \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{n} \\sum_{i=1}^{n} (y_i - \\hat{y}_i)^2}\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(y_i\\) represents the observed values, \\(\\hat{y}_i\\) represents the predicted values, and \\(n\\) is the total number of observations. The RMSE is expressed in the same units as the dependent variable, making it interpretable in the context of the data.\n\n**Design Layout or Structure**:  \nWhile RMSE itself is not an experimental design, it is often used in the context of model evaluation following experimental designs. It can be applied to various types of models, including linear regression, nonlinear models, and machine learning algorithms.\n\n**Assumptions Required for Validity**:  \nThe validity of RMSE as a measure of model accuracy assumes that the errors (the differences between predicted and observed values) are normally distributed and that the model is appropriately specified. Additionally, it assumes that the data is homoscedastic, meaning that the variance of the errors is constant across all levels of the independent variable(s).\n\n**When to Use / Applicability / Selection Criteria**:  \nRMSE is particularly useful when comparing the predictive accuracy of different models or when assessing the performance of a single model. It is applicable in various fields, including engineering, finance, and environmental science, where accurate predictions are critical. RMSE is selected when the goal is to minimize prediction errors, especially in continuous outcome variables.\n\n**Analysis Procedure**:  \nTo compute RMSE, one must first fit a model to the data, generate predictions, and then apply the RMSE formula to evaluate the model's performance. It is often used in conjunction with other metrics, such as Mean Absolute Error (MAE) or R-squared, to provide a comprehensive assessment of model accuracy.\n\n**Advantages and Strengths**:  \nThe primary advantage of RMSE is its sensitivity to large errors, as it squares the differences before averaging, which can highlight significant discrepancies between predicted and observed values. It is straightforward to compute and interpret, making it a popular choice among practitioners.\n\n**Limitations and Disadvantages**:  \nOne limitation of RMSE is that it can be disproportionately influenced by outliers due to the squaring of errors. Additionally, RMSE does not provide information about the direction of the errors (whether predictions are consistently over or under the actual values). It also does not indicate whether a model is biased.\n\n**Related Designs, Methods, or Alternatives**:  \nRMSE is often compared with other error metrics such as Mean Absolute Error (MAE) and Mean Squared Error (MSE). Each of these metrics has its own strengths and weaknesses, and the choice between them may depend on the specific context and goals of the analysis.\n\n**Practical Implementation Considerations**:  \nWhen implementing RMSE, it is important to ensure that the model is properly validated, ideally using techniques such as cross-validation to avoid overfitting. Additionally, practitioners should consider the scale of the data and the implications of using RMSE in the context of the specific application.\n\n**Software Implementation**:  \nRMSE can be easily calculated using statistical software packages such as R, Python (with libraries like scikit-learn), SAS, and MATLAB. Most of these platforms provide built-in functions to compute RMSE directly from model predictions and observed values.\n\n**Common Applications or Examples**:  \nRMSE is widely used in various domains, including but not limited to, forecasting in economics, predictive modeling in machine learning, and performance evaluation in engineering simulations. For instance, in environmental studies, RMSE may be used to assess the accuracy of models predicting pollutant levels based on various input factors.\",5\r\n976,MODEL,\"### Comprehensive Summary of \"\"MODEL\"\"\n\n**Definition and Purpose**  \nA model in the context of statistical analysis and Design of Experiments (DOE) is a mathematical representation that describes the relationship between variables. It serves to make predictions, understand data patterns, and analyze the effects of different factors on a response variable. In regression analysis, models are particularly useful for quantifying relationships and forecasting outcomes based on input variables.\n\n**Mathematical Formulation or Model Structure**  \nMathematically, a model can be expressed in various forms, such as linear or nonlinear equations. A common structure for a linear regression model is given by:\n\n\\[ Y = \\beta_0 + \\beta_1X_1 + \\beta_2X_2 + ... + \\beta_kX_k + \\epsilon \\]\n\nwhere \\( Y \\) is the response variable, \\( \\beta_0 \\) is the intercept, \\( \\beta_1, \\beta_2, ..., \\beta_k \\) are the coefficients for the predictor variables \\( X_1, X_2, ..., X_k \\), and \\( \\epsilon \\) represents the error term.\n\nIn the context of ANOVA, the model can be represented as:\n\n\\[ Y_{ij} = \\mu + \\tau_i + \\epsilon_{ij} \\]\n\nwhere \\( Y_{ij} \\) is the response for the \\( j \\)-th observation in the \\( i \\)-th treatment group, \\( \\mu \\) is the overall mean, \\( \\tau_i \\) is the effect of the \\( i \\)-th treatment, and \\( \\epsilon_{ij} \\) is the random error.\n\n**Design Layout or Structure**  \nIn experimental designs, the model structure is often defined by the layout of the experiment, which includes factors (independent variables), levels (values of factors), and the response variable (dependent variable). The design can be factorial, fractional factorial, or other types depending on the number of factors and levels involved.\n\n**Assumptions Required for Validity**  \nThe validity of a statistical model relies on several assumptions, including:\n- Linearity: The relationship between the independent and dependent variables is linear.\n- Independence: Observations are independent of each other.\n- Homoscedasticity: The variance of the residuals is constant across all levels of the independent variables.\n- Normality: The residuals are normally distributed.\n\n**When to Use / Applicability / Selection Criteria**  \nModels are applicable in various scenarios, particularly when there is a need to understand the relationship between multiple factors and a response variable. Selection criteria include the nature of the data, the number of factors, the complexity of relationships, and the specific objectives of the analysis (e.g., prediction vs. hypothesis testing).\n\n**Analysis Procedure**  \nThe analysis procedure typically involves fitting the model to the data using methods such as least squares estimation for regression models or ANOVA for comparing means across groups. Model diagnostics are then performed to check the validity of the assumptions and the adequacy of the model.\n\n**Advantages and Strengths**  \nModels provide a structured approach to understanding complex relationships in data. They allow for predictions, facilitate hypothesis testing, and can handle multiple factors simultaneously. Additionally, they can be used to identify significant factors affecting the response variable.\n\n**Limitations and Disadvantages**  \nModels can be limited by their assumptions; violations of these assumptions can lead to incorrect conclusions. Overfitting can occur if a model is too complex relative to the amount of data available. Additionally, models may not capture all relevant factors or interactions, leading to biased results.\n\n**Related Designs, Methods, or Alternatives**  \nRelated methods include regression analysis, ANOVA, mixed models, and generalized linear models. Alternatives may involve non-parametric methods or machine learning techniques when traditional assumptions are not met.\n\n**Practical Implementation Considerations**  \nWhen implementing models, practitioners should consider data collection methods, sample size, and the potential for confounding variables. It is also essential to conduct exploratory data analysis prior to modeling to understand the data structure and relationships.\n\n**Software Implementation**  \nModels can be implemented using various statistical software packages, including R, SAS, SPSS, and Python libraries such as scikit-learn and statsmodels. These tools provide functions for model fitting, diagnostics, and visualization.\n\n**Common Applications or Examples**  \nCommon applications of models include predicting outcomes in clinical trials, understanding consumer behavior in marketing studies, and optimizing processes in manufacturing. For example, a model may be used to predict yield based on factors such as temperature, pressure, and time in a chemical process. In ANOVA contexts, models are frequently employed to compare the effects of different treatments on a response variable, such as the impact of different fertilizers on crop yield.\",22\r\n1412,TABLE 6.7,\"Table 6.7 presents the analysis of variance table for the selected factorial model, summarizing the results of the ANOVA.\",3\r\n14